🔹 Question 2: Subsequence After One Replacement
Problem: Given strings s and t, can s become a subsequence of t after at most one replacement in s?

Initial Thoughts:

Hashmap idea rejected → subsequence depends on order, not frequency.

Two‑pointer approach is natural: i on s, j on t.

Optimized Approach:

Walk through both strings:

If match → move both.

If mismatch:

If replacement not used → either replace now OR skip in t.

If replacement already used → must skip in t.

At end, check i == s.size().

Bug Found:

Greedy “replace at first mismatch” fails for cases like s="ws", t="xwxt".

Fix: Try both strategies → replace early OR skip first. If either succeeds, return true.

Final Code:

cpp
bool canmake(string s, string t) {
    int n = s.size(), m = t.size();

    auto checkPath = [&](bool replaceEarly) {
        int i = 0, j = 0;
        bool used = false;
        while (i < n && j < m) {
            if (s[i] == t[j]) { i++; j++; }
            else {
                if (!used) {
                    if (replaceEarly) { used = true; i++; j++; }
                    else { j++; }
                } else j++;
            }
        }
        return i == n;
    };

    return checkPath(true) || checkPath(false);
}
Key Insights:

Subsequence allows unlimited skips in t, replacement only once in s.

DP framing possible (dp[i][j][used]), but overkill → greedy + flag suffices.

Interview hook: “Looks like DP, but collapses into linear two‑pointer with one flag.”

